\import{spin-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{real types are not Schur indices}
\tag{math}
\tag{fgap}
\tag{group-algebra}
\tag{representation}

\p{Over #{\mathbb R}, a simple finite-dimensional block may instead have
division algebra #{\mathbb R}, #{\mathbb C}, or #{\mathbb H}. The
Frobenius--Schur indicator detects the real, complex, or quaternionic type of
a complex irreducible character, but it is not a complete theorem about its
field of values or Schur index. The explicit decomposition
##{
\mathbb R[Q_8]\cong\mathbb R^4\times\mathbb H
}
in [[fgap-001B]] is a complete real example, not an application of the
algebraically closed formula. For Schur indices and invariant forms, see
\citet{def. 23.13, thms. 23.14 and 23.16, and cor. 23.17, pp. 273--278}{james2001representations}
and \citet{sec. 2.9, pp. 164--172}{lux2010representations}.}
